      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND AIM",
          "content": "To analyze plasma Lp(a) levels and examine different risk factors and coronary heart disease (CHD) in a sample of genetically diagnosed familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND RESULTS",
          "content": "Ninety heterozygous FH patients and 41 non-FH relatives were enrolled in a study to evaluate their plasma and lipoprotein cholesterol, as well as their triglyceride and Lp(a) levels. We found no differences in plasma Lp(a) levels and log transformed values between 90 FH subjects and their 41 unaffected relatives (22.3 mg/dl +/- 19.4 vs 17.7 mg/dl +/- 21.3 and 1.12 +/- 0.5 vs 0.96 +/- 0.54) nor between null allele and defective allele FH subjects (log Lp (a) levels 2.013 +/- 0.282 vs 1.959 +/- 0.151). FH CHD+ were significantly older, and had higher mean systolic and diastolic blood pressure and higher mean plasma triglyceride levels than FH CHD-. No differences in mean and log transformed Lp(a) plasma concentrations were found."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Plasma Lp(a) levels are not related to LDL receptor status and class mutations, nor to the presence of CHD in FH patients."
        }
      ]},
